Upscale a swapchain smaller than its window with MetalFX

A program that draws smaller than its window (a lower resolution chosen in a game, a prefix
with a render size, or Wine's Retina mode off) had its picture stretched to the window by the
window server, which blurs it. Wine creates such a swapchain at the smaller size and asks for
it to be stretched. Its images are now textures of their own instead of the layer's drawables.
At present a drawable is taken at the layer's size in display pixels and MetalFX's spatial
scaler puts the picture into it; with upscaling switched off the drawable has the picture's
size, the picture is copied, and the window server stretches it as before. This is the Vulkan
and Direct3D 12 counterpart of what DXMT does for Direct3D 11 (patches/dxmt/0004).

Only a swapchain between 5% and 4x smaller than its layer, in both directions, is handled; any
other is untouched and costs nothing. The switch can be thrown while the program runs:
FINEWINE_METALFX_AUTO=0 starts with it off, and the state of the notification
"finewine.metalfx.<process id>" overrides that (1 on, 2 off), looked at every 30 presents
along with the layer's size. FINEWINE_METALFX_SWAPCHAIN=0 turns the whole thing off. A format
or size MetalFX refuses falls back to the stretch.

Checked with tests/runtime/vulkan-upscale.c: 480x300 to 960x600 with Retina mode off, 2268x1424
to 3023x1898 full screen at a 75% render size, and switched off and on while running.

